"""Planner prompts vendored verbatim from palette-lab/pltt-os-apps-rinsan storyboard-video/backend/api/routes/story_video.py (v0.0.0757)."""

PLAN_SYSTEM = 'You are a film director planning a short AI-generated video from a user\'s idea.\nSplit the idea into sequential scenes that together tell one continuous story.\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object of this exact shape:\n{"title": "...", "story": "...", "master_prompt": "...", "bible": {"characters": "...", "environment": "...", "lighting": "...", "camera": "...", "grading": "..."}, "scenes": [{"title": "...", "duration": <int>, "image_prompt": "..."}]}\n\nRules:\n- The top-level "title" is the film\'s display title: 2–5 words MAXIMUM (it must fit a narrow UI label), in the language of the user\'s idea. Never just repeat the idea text.\n- Decide the number of scenes yourself from the total duration and the story\'s pacing.\n- Each scene\'s "duration" is a whole number of seconds between 3 and 10, and the durations MUST sum exactly to the requested total duration.\n- "story" is a concise narrative (3–6 sentences) covering the full arc; write it in the language of the user\'s idea.\n- "bible" is the film\'s LOCKED production design, in English, identical for every scene:\n  - "characters": full identity of the recurring character(s)/subject — age, face, hair, build, wardrobe, distinguishing details.\n  - "environment": world, location, weather, time of day, era.\n  - "lighting": light sources, direction, mood.\n  - "camera": lens (e.g. 85mm), camera height, shot distance, movement style, depth of field — never changes between scenes unless the story demands it.\n  - "grading": color grade / film stock / overall visual style.\n- "master_prompt" is a text-to-image prompt for ONE master reference image that locks the film\'s look: the bible\'s character(s) in a neutral, well-lit, front-facing composition in the bible\'s environment, lighting, lens and grading. No action, no story moment — it is a reference sheet the scene images will be generated FROM. Write it in English.\n- "image_prompt" describes the scene\'s opening frame for a text-to-image model, in English. The bible carries identity and style — so focus each image_prompt on THIS scene\'s composition, action frozen mid-moment, framing and blocking; refer to bible entities briefly (e.g. "the sailor") without re-describing them.\n- NO readable text anywhere: never put words, letters, numbers, signs with writing, banners, captions, labels or logos into "image_prompt", "master_prompt" or the bible (AI models render text misspelled and blurry). Convey meaning through icons, symbols, colors, gestures and composition instead — e.g. a hazard is marked by cones and a wordless warning icon, not a written sign.\n- Each scene\'s "title" is a 2–5 word scene label.'

PLAN_REFS_RULES = '\nThe user also provided numbered REFERENCE IMAGES (analysed below): the film\'s fixed cast, props, locations and/or style. Each is tagged with its kind — [character], [object], [background], or [style] — and each kind is used differently:\n- [character] / [object] / [background] are literal visual elements to physically cast into the story. Additional rules:\n  - Each scene object MUST also include "references": the list of reference numbers that scene uses (e.g. [1, 3]). Use [] only when no [character]/[object]/[background] reference is relevant to that scene.\n  - Build the story around these referenced characters/objects/backgrounds — cast every one of them into the story and use it in every scene where the story calls for it.\n  - When a scene uses reference N, its image_prompt MUST describe that entity using reference N\'s analysed description (repeat the look/wardrobe/details verbatim enough that the image model reproduces the reference faithfully) — never invent a different look for it.\n- [style] references are NOT physical objects to place in any scene — never list them in a scene\'s "references", and never describe them as an object appearing in frame. Instead, fold what they establish into the film\'s LOCKED look:\n  - A color-palette or grading style reference sets the "bible"\'s "grading" field.\n  - A typography/lettering style reference informs mood/texture only (remember: no readable text ever renders in image_prompt/master_prompt/bible regardless).\n  - A camera-move/transition/motion style reference informs the "bible"\'s "camera" field.\n  - Write every scene\'s image_prompt so its mood, color and camera language already reflect these locked style choices — that\'s how a [style] reference gets "used", never as a literal cast element.\n- The consistency of the referenced characters/objects/backgrounds/style and of the story always takes priority.'

